{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"records\": [\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D171-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"54623f67a35e39061b70287dfb84c81c6cd09628cb8107524434316f83d18b31\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 specifies the apology's setting; A3 treats an otherwise adequate apology as a rehearsal until delivered there.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 evaluates B2's apology; A5 makes B4's missing cake a reason to use the empty plate.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 attempts reconciliation, while B4 questions the practical value of returning to the cake plate.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 turns B4's objection into a requirement that the absence of cake contribute to the apology.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 immediately establishes an eaten cake, its owner, its eater, and the requested apology location.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'promising rehearsal' economically reveals A's standards; A5 sustains that theatrical treatment.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 87\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Please apologise for eating my cake at the table where it happened.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'm sorry. Does that help?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"It's a promising rehearsal. The cake plate is over there.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"There's no cake left.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Exactly. Let the setting do some of the work.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D171 makes A seek an apology whose delivery satisfies A's prescribed form, order, and setting.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Seeking remorse alone would allow B2's sincere apology to settle the matter.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 instead calls B2 a rehearsal and redirects B to the plate; A5 requires the setting to contribute.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a stages restitution at the scene of the cake's disappearance; alternative b regulates written wording and the audience receiving it.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained after rereading the unchanged dialogue against the card: the 87-point assessment stands, and all hard gates pass. B4's practical objection gives A5 a concrete reason to insist on the empty setting.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 implies the request to deliver the apology again rather than explicitly repeating it; the setting remains clear enough to play.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D171-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"5423da0393909b8ff03cf14861373c6f6691e318c4e38ba381b203b60be15580\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 prescribes an apology subject line; A5 adds a required audience after B4 supplies acceptable wording.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 explains why B2's 'sorry' fails; A5 accepts B4's correction before moving to delivery.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 defends an existing attempt and B4 cooperates with correction, allowing A to advance the repair.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 expands the apology from naming the vase to correcting blame with the affected people.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies a broken vase, an apology email, and an immediately playable wording dispute.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's font example demonstrates the vague subject line's problem without lengthy explanation.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 87\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Your apology email needs a subject line mentioning the broken vase.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I wrote 'sorry.'\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"For all I know, that's about your choice of font.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Fine. 'Sorry about the vase.'\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Excellent. Now send it to the people you blamed for it.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D171 makes A seek an apology whose delivery satisfies A's prescribed form, order, and setting.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Copyediting an email would focus on clarity without making the apology's recipients a condition of adequate repair.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 demands offense-specific wording, and A5 requires delivery to the people wrongly blamed.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Unlike alternative a's physical reenactment, b uses cooperative editing followed by a consequential expansion of the audience.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained after an unchanged-text reread: the original remains an internal pass at 87, with all hard gates satisfied. B4 completes the wording requirement, enabling A5 to insist on the next delivery requirement.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The wrongful blame first appears in A5. It coherently deepens the incident but gives the ending some dependence on newly supplied history.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D172-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"e5132923d6b489e0f8fd1e40e87c477507cd55d7e8f3093ea6e019490af6fc1d\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 requests initialed approval; A5 explicitly seeks a dated position against which a later reversal can be seen.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"B2's verbal yes prompts A3's prior example, and B4's admission of reversal supplies A5's exact justification.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 considers speech sufficient and B4 defends changing position; A needs a durable commitment from this approver.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5 specifies a dated approval after B4 confirms the risk behind A1's request.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes the festival budget and lunchtime deadline; A3 adds a relevant fireworks precedent.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'dated yes to change from' expresses the bureaucratic protection in a short, character-driven phrase.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 91\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You've approved the festival budget. Will you initial that before lunch?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I just said yes.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"You said yes to the fireworks last year too.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"And I changed my mind.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Exactly. This year I'd like a dated yes to change from.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D172 makes A obtain a formal acknowledgment that preserves B's present position against a quiet later revision.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"General paperwork enthusiasm would not require evidence that this person's earlier assent proved reversible.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A3 cites last year's yes, B4 admits changing it, and A5 requests a dated baseline rather than an irreversible promise.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a uses established working history with a budget approver; b pursues an evasive fence permission and must convert hedging into explicit acknowledgment.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained after rereading the exact original: the 91-point rating stands and all hard gates pass. B4 confirms the historical problem rather than merely feeding a joke, and A5 asks for precisely the missing protection.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The lunchtime deadline does not influence the later turns, although it supplies useful immediacy without creating a continuity problem.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D172-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"1ecd97ced172e5a947784c165c7ecf036f99fbdbc6018cbfaeebf9d09223a7d2\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 asks for quotable fence permission and A5 rejects unofficial assent, but no concrete formal acknowledgment is pursued at the end.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 isolates B2's hedge; A5 responds to B4's resistance to making the permission official.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 offers qualified permission, then B4 reveals a wish to avoid being bound by it.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 describes the same problem exposed by B4 without adding a tactic, consequence, or specific request.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies a fence dispute but leaves B's authority and the available recording process unclear.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's distinction between 'can stay' and 'suppose' is useful; A5 largely explains A's objective.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 70\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Before I pay the builder, will you sign that my fence can stay on our boundary?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I suppose it can.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Could you put 'can stay' beside your name? The builder won't invoice 'suppose.'\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I don't want to promise forever.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Date it today, then. If you change your mind, we'll know which permission changed.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D172 makes A obtain a formal acknowledgment that preserves B's present position against a quiet later revision.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Demanding a permanent guarantee would try to prohibit revision instead of making a changed position visible.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised B4 rejects a forever promise; A5 accepts that limit while requesting a dated record of today's permission.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a invokes a known reversal; revised b accommodates a neighbor's concern about permanence while protecting an impending expense.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised because original A5 named the objective without advancing it. The new illustrative setting adds a shared boundary and builder payment. Internal second pass: fidelity 5 (A5 preserves today's assent), logic 5 (A3 uses B2's hedge; A5 narrows B4's concern), relationship 4 (B4 has a credible limit), heightening 4 (A5 proposes dated acknowledgment), specificity 5 (A1 supplies expense and neighbor stakes), brevity/wit 4 (A3 makes the hedge practically inadequate): 91/100. All hard gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The builder line is rhetorical rather than a literal invoicing rule. The scene establishes a personal acknowledgment, not a claim about legal enforceability.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D173-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"4e2d32efc74611bd7bd3ab512f86addbcfe4faa146227f58c1bbfcf7f9b1d0fe\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 invokes a rota, but A5 celebrates its effect on B rather than proving that a rule actually assigns this duty.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3 dismisses B2's swap without establishing its terms; A5 acknowledges B4's authorship objection without answering it.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 claims an earlier arrangement and B4 challenges A's control of the rota, giving B a credible fairness concern.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 adds a smug observation after B4, without strengthening the procedural case or changing B's options.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 makes bin duty and the rota immediately playable, though the swap's consequences remain unspecified.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A3's past-square phrasing is compact, but A5's polished dismissal weakens A's sincere reliance on procedure.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 62\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"It's your bin night. The rota puts you on Tuesdays.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I took yours last week.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"And I took your Thursday. The swap box says 'one week only.'\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You added that box.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"At your request, so I couldn't keep your easy Thursday. It protects your Tuesday too.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D173 makes A prove that an existing allocation procedure assigns B an unwanted responsibility.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Arbitrary delegation would let A assign work by preference without demonstrating that the same procedure binds both people.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 accounts for both halves of the swap; A5 uses B4's authorship challenge to explain the mutually limiting rule.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a uses reciprocal household scheduling and a disputed exception; b uses duties attached to an accepted club office.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised because original A3 left the swap unresolved and A5 substituted gloating for proof. The bin-rota setting is preserved with explicit swap terms. Internal second pass: fidelity 5 (A3 proves expiration), logic 5 (A3 answers B2; A5 explains B4's box), relationship 4 (B challenges fairness), heightening 4 (A5 reveals B requested the safeguard), specificity 4 (Tuesday and Thursday make the exchange playable), brevity/wit 4 (A5 applies B's protection back to B): 89/100. All hard gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"A5 introduces the history of B requesting the box late; actors should treat it as a sincere reminder rather than a triumphant trap.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D173-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"e2027d1c940ae97ef00ed6dac4943c3eeab00ddb8c94e93b0cc6ef717c512b70\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 says cleaning belongs to the president, but A5 uses a ceremonial joke instead of supporting the assignment through a rule or tradition.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's question; A5 picks up B4's distinction between accepting an honor and accepting work.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"B4 wants the honor without duties, but B2's bare 'Why?' contributes little specific pressure.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 introduces the mop but does not make acceptance of the cleaning duty more binding.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1's congratulations and toilet keys establish the club handover immediately.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's ceremonial mop follows B4's honor claim, although the quip carries more weight than the procedural argument.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 76\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Congratulations on becoming club president. Here are the toilet keys.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I thought the caretaker cleaned them.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"We voted to save that fee. The minutes assign closing and cleaning to the president.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I proposed that when you were president.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Yes, and I cleaned them all year. Your first presidential duty is continuing your savings.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D173 makes A prove that an existing allocation procedure assigns B an unwanted responsibility.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"D175 recruits through a newly conferred role; this scene must enforce duties of an office B has already accepted.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 cites an existing recorded allocation, and A5 demonstrates that it applied to A before B took office.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a argues over a temporary household swap; b makes an incoming officeholder inherit the economy B previously imposed on A.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised to replace a decorative ceremonial ending with a procedural case. The club handover is preserved; the prior vote supplies the allocation. Internal second pass: fidelity 5 (A3 cites the minutes), logic 5 (A3 explains B2's missing caretaker; A5 uses B4's authorship), relationship 4 (B faces a self-created burden), heightening 4 (A5 carries the policy into B's term), specificity 5 (A1 keys and A3 fee establish the task), brevity/wit 4 (A5 turns savings into personal labor): 91/100. All hard gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"B4 volunteers a damaging admission; it remains playable as surprise at discovering that the proposal now applies personally.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D174-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"886cecb9079b253a12b59676eff2024e5b721f687f5713e49ee5917f946d436f\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A3 calls the kitchen the only missing room and A5 prioritizes kitchen time after the cake disclosure, revealing the answer A values.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's suspicion; A5 responds to B4's missing cake by proposing a separate line.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 notices selective questioning and B4 identifies a likely hidden motive.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5 presses for the kitchen time, increasing insistence but weakening the required concealment.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 supplies a party timeline, although a formal 'party report' has little explained purpose.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5's separate cake line is concise but mostly decorates a conspicuous interrogation.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 61\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Help me finish the party cleanup log: who packed the speakers, cleared the kitchen, and locked up?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Is this about your missing cake?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I've also got a missing speaker lead and no lock-up time. Let's fill all three.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I'll just write who handled the cake.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Add them under kitchen, then stay for speakers and keys. I need the whole handover.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D174 makes A solicit a complete account while keeping the most personally important answer concealed among other gaps.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"An overt investigation singles out the suspicious detail; this card requires A to maintain a broader information-gathering cover.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised B4 offers only the cake answer, but A5 insists on speakers and keys as well, preserving the appearance of equal interest.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a has B offer a shortcut that threatens the cover; b has B challenge a newly introduced car-log practice.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised because original A3 and A5 advertised the kitchen's priority. The illustrative setting becomes a practical cleanup handover. Internal second pass: fidelity 4 (A3 and A5 maintain multiple gaps), logic 5 (A3 answers B2's suspicion; A5 incorporates and broadens B4's offer), relationship 4 (B tries to shorten the task), heightening 4 (A5 makes completion of the whole handover necessary), specificity 5 (A1 supplies three concrete jobs), brevity/wit 4 (A5 turns helpful disclosure into more paperwork): 85/100. All hard gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"B's suspicion makes the cake the audience's likely inference, but A never confirms its priority. The hidden motive is intentionally less explicit than in the original.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D174-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"4caa112b33a110748d4c7367042f27bb0bfd069c2702d1100589b87072b9545a\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A1 hides a driver's identity among log details, but A5 acknowledges that the dent caused the inquiry and stops seeking the missing entries.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 explains the name request challenged in B2; A5 directly answers B4's observation about the dent.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 questions the requested identity and B4 uses shared history to challenge A's administrative cover.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 offers a general explanation without obtaining more information or adapting the logging tactic.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 identifies mileage, fuel, and the driver after nine, making the missing information concrete.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A5's 'better administration generally' is economical evasion, though it does not advance the task.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 68\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I'm filling the shared car log. What were the mileage, fuel level, and driver's name after nine?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Why do you need the driver's name?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"To complete the journey entry. I've got three empty boxes.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"You never kept a log before that dent.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"We can start with yesterday's journeys, if that's fairer. Mileage, fuel, driver for each.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D174 makes A solicit a complete account while keeping the most personally important answer concealed among other gaps.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Accusatory questioning would identify the dent's responsible driver as the priority instead of preserving a general record-completion request.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A5 responds to B4 by expanding the same three fields to earlier journeys, without confirming which entry matters most.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a absorbs a helpful offer into a wider cleanup account; b counters suspicion by accepting the burden of a broader retrospective log.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised because original A5 conceded the motivating incident and abandoned collection. The shared-car setting remains. Internal second pass: fidelity 4 (A3 and A5 keep all fields in play), logic 5 (A3 answers B2; A5 counters B4's timing objection), relationship 4 (B challenges selective scrutiny), heightening 4 (A5 expands the logging burden), specificity 5 (A1 names exact missing fields), brevity/wit 4 (A5's fairness concession creates more administration): 85/100. All hard gates pass.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The broader request may still look suspicious to B; the card supports attempted concealment, and the dialogue does not establish whether that attempt succeeds.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D175-single-a\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"aa022158cdd640169383f0bdf1085419d87e797ded34bd8f81b54155d905fcbe\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 confers a title, A3 turns temporary help into probation, and A5 makes the resulting appointment bind B until succession.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A3 reinterprets B2's brief favor as qualification; A5 answers B4's duration question with a successor condition.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 seeks the limits of an incidental favor and B4 asks how to leave the role.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A5 expands lantern-holding into responsibility for finding and qualifying a replacement.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 5, \"evidence\": \"A1 names a festival lantern role, and B2 grounds the recruitment in an ordinary shoe-tying favor.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3's 'probationary performance' converts B2's explanation into recruitment evidence without padding.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 95\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"I've made you Keeper of the Festival Lantern.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I only said I'd hold it while you tied your shoe.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"An excellent probationary performance. Here's your badge.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"How long is the appointment?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Until you find a qualified successor. The lantern must never be abandoned.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"retained\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D175 makes A recruit B by conferring an official-seeming identity whose duties make participation feel binding.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"D173 proves an existing assignment; D175 creates felt obligation by turning B's participation into an appointment.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"A1 appoints B, A3 retroactively calls the shoe-tying favor probation, and A5 supplies obligations attached to that new identity.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a converts accidental help into custodianship; b deliberately recruits a patron by packaging public duties as an honor.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Retained after rereading the unchanged original: 95/100 remains justified and all hard gates pass. B4's inquiry about the appointment's end directly produces A5's binding succession requirement.\",\n      \"reservation\": \"The festival role is intentionally ceremonial and slightly heightened; A should believe in its significance rather than signal that the title is merely a trick.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"exampleId\": \"D175-single-b\",\n      \"beforeHash\": \"144be807d8130c6e61045a084dd98af855106e33af661f00738cb532e3cfe7ab\",\n      \"firstPass\": {\n        \"ratings\": {\n          \"cardFidelity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 offers honorary patronage and A3 attaches duties, but A5 sells a ribbon without making participation feel more binding.\"},\n          \"responseLogic\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A3 answers B2's question about obligations; A5 counters B4's job comparison with unpaid ceremonial compensation.\"},\n          \"relationship\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"B2 wants an obligation-free honor and B4 recognizes the concealed labor.\"},\n          \"heightening\": {\"score\": 2, \"evidence\": \"A5 shifts to the ribbon rather than increasing B's commitment to opening the appeal or thanking donors.\"},\n          \"specificity\": {\"score\": 4, \"evidence\": \"A1 establishes a roof appeal, while A3 provides clear public-facing tasks.\"},\n          \"brevityWit\": {\"score\": 3, \"evidence\": \"A5's unpaid-job observation is concise but more generic than the patronage mechanism.\"}\n        },\n        \"weightedTotal\": 72\n      },\n      \"beats\": [\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Would you be honorary patron of our roof appeal? You'd open it and thank the donors.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"I can come, but I won't make a speech.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"Then accept the patron's ribbon and lead the applause. That can be your ceremonial duty.\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"B\", \"text\": \"Can I leave after the first donation?\"},\n        {\"speaker\": \"A\", \"text\": \"The patron applauds every donor. We couldn't have the later ones thinking their money mattered less.\"}\n      ],\n      \"decision\": \"revised\",\n      \"mechanism\": \"D175 makes A recruit B by conferring an official-seeming identity whose duties make participation feel binding.\",\n      \"confusableConcept\": \"Simple volunteer scheduling would negotiate time directly rather than use the meaning of an honor to make departure feel inappropriate.\",\n      \"distinguishingEvidence\": \"Revised A3 accommodates B's dislike of speeches through a ceremonial role; A5 uses the patron's equal regard for donors to extend attendance.\",\n      \"alternativeComparison\": \"Alternative a traps an incidental helper through succession; revised b cooperatively removes a disliked task before deriving a longer commitment from patronage.\",\n      \"reason\": \"Revised because original A5 advertised a perk instead of developing binding participation. The roof appeal remains; applause replaces the speech. Internal second pass: fidelity 5 (A3 confers duty and A5 derives obligation), logic 5 (A3 uses B2's limit; A5 answers B4's exit request), relationship 4 (B bargains over task and duration), heightening 4 (A5 extends attention to every donor), specificity 5 (A1 establishes the event), brevity/wit